Vorg the Sapient wrote...

> It's one thing to overcome any dependence on good luck, but more
> difficult is avoiding bad luck.  A monster spawned with an especially
> dangerous wand, for example, or being hit by a poisonous dart, or
> blown up when a gas spore steps onto a falling rock trap right next to
> you.

But those things happen extremely infrequently.  Out of the past
thirty or so games I've played (most played for at least 10,000 turns)
I can only recall two wand-deaths, one "deadly poison", and no gas
spores on rock traps.

Conversely, out of those games, I can recall at least a dozen in which
I found one, two or even three magic lamps, probably 15 in which I
found artifact weapons randomly or in bones files, 10 or 12 where I
found a bag of holding outside of Sokoban, at least 5 in which I got a
wish from a throne, and two in which I found randomly-generated wands
of wishing.

Overall, I very much agree these days that the effect of luck can be
severely minimized in NetHack, which only adds to the proof that NH is
the most well-balanced of all the roguelikes.

Also, I think people tend to remember the annoying instances of bad
luck in an uneven proportion to how often they actually occur.

